  <h2 id="later">Where you will meet this again</h2>
  <Callout kind="note" label="Optional preview">
    <p>
      A look ahead. Every unfamiliar word links to the glossary; skip this on a first read and come back after week 8.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Week 3, penalising big weights.</strong> The exact solution of regression with a penalty is
      <Math tex="(X^\top X + \lambda I)^{-1} X^\top \mathbf{y}" />: transposes, a product, the identity matrix.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Week 5, neural networks.</strong> A <Term id="layer">layer</Term> is <Math tex="XW^\top + \mathbf{b}" />
      followed by a bend, and a network is the shape table above with more rows. PyTorch (week 6) calls the step
      <code>nn.Linear</code> and stores the weight as (outputs, inputs), exactly as here.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Week 7, embeddings.</strong> A language model’s lookup table is <Math tex="V \times d" /> (50,257 × 768
      for GPT-2). Looking up a <Term id="token">token</Term> is reading one row, which is the same as multiplying a
      one-hot row of length <Math tex="V" /> by the matrix.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Week 8, attention.</strong> <Term id="attention">Attention</Term> makes three stacks of vectors with
      three weight matrices, forms the <Math tex="n \times n" /> matrix of Fig. 5 (called <Math tex="QK^\top" />),
      turns each row into percentages, and multiplies by the third stack to get <Math tex="n \times d" /> again.
      Three matmuls, and the shape rule tells you the shape at every step. Because the middle matrix is
      <Math tex="n \times n" />, attention costs grow with the <em>square</em> of the text length.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Week 10, cost tricks.</strong> The <Term id="kv-cache">KV cache</Term> is two of those stacks kept in
      memory so they are not recomputed; its size is a shape calculation. <Term id="lora">LoRA</Term> adds a product of
      two thin matrices <Math tex="BA" /> to a frozen weight and uses associativity to apply it cheaply. And parameter
      counts are sums of <Math tex="mn + m" /> over the weight matrices, priced at 2 FLOPs per parameter per token.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="interview">The full interview answer</h2>
  <p>
    The typical form is a shape question dressed up as something else: “walk me through the shapes when a batch goes
    through a weight matrix” or, after week 8, “what is the shape of the attention scores and why”. Here is a complete
    answer in about seventy seconds.
  </p>
  <Callout kind="interview">
    <p>
      “A weight matrix is stored as (outputs, inputs) with a bias of shape (outputs,). For a batch the input is
      (batch, inputs), and we compute <Math tex="XW^\top + \mathbf{b}" />: the inner input dimensions match and cancel,
      giving (batch, outputs), with the bias broadcast across the rows. Each entry of the result is the dot product of
      one example with one output’s weight vector. Because matmul only touches the last axis, the same step works on
      (batch, seq, features) when each example is a sequence. Matmul is associative but not commutative, so order is
      fixed but bracketing is free, and the cheapest bracketing can be hundreds of times cheaper. Each matmul costs
      about 2mnp FLOPs, so running a model once is roughly 2 FLOPs per parameter per token, and that is why GPUs,
      which run the independent dot products in parallel, are the hardware of choice.”
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>After week 8 you can add:</em> “In attention, queries and keys are both (seq, d), and <Math tex="QK^\top" />
      is (seq, d)(d, seq) = (seq, seq), whose entry <Math tex="(i, j)" /> is the dot product of query <Math tex="i" />
      with key <Math tex="j" />. Multiplying the normalised scores by <Math tex="V" />, (seq, seq)(seq, d), gives
      (seq, d) again, and the <Math tex="n \times n" /> middle is why attention is quadratic in sequence length.”
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p><strong>If they push further, be ready for:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>“Why (outputs, inputs) and not (inputs, outputs)?” Convention from writing <Math tex="W\mathbf{x}" /> with column vectors; one row per output. With examples as rows you apply the transpose.</li>
    <li>“How many parameters in a layer from 768 inputs to 3072 outputs?” <Math tex="768 \times 3072 + 3072 = 2{,}362{,}368" />.</li>
    <li>“Is matmul commutative?” No. Associative and distributive, yes. Give the thin-matrices bracketing example.</li>
    <li>“What is the difference between <code>*</code> and <code>@</code>?” Element-wise product versus matrix product.</li>
    <li>“Why is <Term id="attention">attention</Term> quadratic in sequence length?” (Week 8.) Its score matrix is <Math tex="n \times n" />: <Math tex="n^2 d" /> work and <Math tex="n^2" /> memory.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="questions">Practice questions</h2>
  <p>Answer each one out loud before opening it.</p>
  <QA q="What is the shape of A B if A is 5 × 3 and B is 3 × 7? And B A?">
    <p><Math tex="AB" /> is <Math tex="5 \times 7" />. <Math tex="BA" /> is <Math tex="(3 \times 7)(5 \times 3)" />: inner 7 and 5 do not match, so it is not defined.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="In one sentence, what is entry (i, j) of A B?">
    <p>The dot product of row <Math tex="i" /> of <Math tex="A" /> with column <Math tex="j" /> of <Math tex="B" />.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="A step takes 20 input features to 5 outputs. What are the shapes of its weight matrix and bias, and how many parameters does it have?">
    <p>Weight <code>(5, 20)</code>, bias <code>(5,)</code>. Parameters <Math tex="5 \times 20 + 5 = 105" />.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="An array of shape (16, 50, 20) goes through that 20-to-5 step. What comes out?">
    <p><code>(16, 50, 5)</code>. Only the last axis changes.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Why do we compute X Wᵀ rather than X W?">
    <p>Because <Math tex="W" /> is stored as (outputs, inputs) with one row per output, and the batch <Math tex="X" /> is (batch, inputs). Row-with-row dot products need the second matrix flipped so its rows become columns: <Math tex="(\text{batch}, \text{in})(\text{in}, \text{out})" />.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Is matrix multiplication commutative? Associative? Give one practical consequence of each answer.">
    <p>Not commutative: swapping changes the result or the shape, so formulas must keep their order. Associative: you may choose the bracketing, so for two thin matrices you compute <Math tex="B(A\mathbf{x})" /> instead of <Math tex="(BA)\mathbf{x}" /> and save hundreds of times the work.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="What is (A B)ᵀ?">
    <p><Math tex="B^\top A^\top" />. The order reverses; check with shapes.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="A and B are both (n, d). What is A Bᵀ, what is its entry (i, j), and why does the cost grow with the square of n?">
    <p><Math tex="n \times n" />; entry <Math tex="(i, j)" /> is <Math tex="\mathbf{a}_i \cdot \mathbf{b}_j" />; there are <Math tex="n^2" /> entries, each costing <Math tex="d" /> operations, so time and memory grow with the square of <Math tex="n" />. (This is why attention, in week 8, is “quadratic in sequence length”.)</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="How many FLOPs is a matmul of (m × n) by (n × p), and what rule of thumb follows for running a model once?">
    <p>About <Math tex="2mnp" />. A weight matrix with <Math tex="mn" /> parameters costs <Math tex="2mn" /> FLOPs per input vector, so running the model once is roughly 2 FLOPs per parameter per token.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="What is the difference between A * B and A @ B in NumPy?">
    <p><code>A * B</code> multiplies matching entries (element-wise, same shape in or out). <code>A @ B</code> is matrix multiplication (dot products of rows with columns, shapes must chain).</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Can you add an array of shape (4,) to one of shape (4, 2)? What about (2,)?">
    <p>(4,) fails: aligned from the right, 4 is compared with 2. (2,) works: it is broadcast as one copy per row. To add per-row values reshape to (4, 1).</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="What do the columns of a matrix tell you about the function x ↦ M x?">
    <p>They are where the basis vectors land. Column 1 is <Math tex="M" /> applied to (1, 0, …), column 2 to (0, 1, …), and every other input goes to the corresponding linear combination of the columns.</p>
  </QA>

  <h2 id="practice">Practice problems</h2>
  <p>Paper first. Write shapes before numbers, every time. Solutions are hidden under each problem.</p>
  <h3>By hand</h3>
  <QA q="1. For each pair of shapes, say whether A B is defined and give its shape: (2,3)(3,4); (3,4)(2,3); (1,5)(5,1); (5,1)(1,5); (4,4)(4,4); (6,2)(2,2).">
    <p>
      (2,4); not defined (4 ≠ 2); (1,1), a single number; (5,5); (4,4); (6,2).
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="2. Compute A B and B A for A = [[1, 2], [3, 4]] and B = [[2, 0], [1, 2]].">
    <p>
      <Math tex="AB" />: row 1 of A with columns of B: <Math tex="(2 + 2, 0 + 4) = (4, 4)" />; row 2: <Math tex="(6 + 4, 0 + 8) = (10, 8)" />.
      So <Math tex="AB = [[4, 4], [10, 8]]" />. <Math tex="BA" />: row 1 of B: <Math tex="(2 + 0, 4 + 0) = (2, 4)" />; row 2: <Math tex="(1 + 6, 2 + 8) = (7, 10)" />.
      So <Math tex="BA = [[2, 4], [7, 10]]" />. Different, as expected.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="3. Multiply A = [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]] by the column (1, 0, −1). Then read the answer as a combination of A’s columns.">
    <p>
      Row 1: <Math tex="1 + 0 - 3 = -2" />; row 2: <Math tex="4 + 0 - 6 = -2" />. Result <Math tex="(-2, -2)" />.
      As columns: <Math tex="1 \cdot (1, 4) + 0 \cdot (2, 5) - 1 \cdot (3, 6) = (-2, -2)" />.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="4. Write the transpose of [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]] and of the column vector (7, 8, 9). What are their shapes?">
    <p>
      <Math tex="[[1, 4], [2, 5], [3, 6]]" />, shape 3 × 2. The column (3 × 1) becomes the row <Math tex="(7, 8, 9)" />, shape 1 × 3.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="5. Three walk days X = [[1,1,1], [0,1,0], [1,0,0]], weights W = [[3,−2,−4],[1,−1,0]], bias b = (−1, 0). Compute Z = X Wᵀ + b with its shape.">
    <p>
      Shape (3 × 3)(3 × 2) = 3 × 2. Day 1 (1,1,1): score 1: <Math tex="3 - 2 - 4 - 1 = -4" />; score 2: <Math tex="1 - 1 + 0 = 0" />.
      Day 2 (0,1,0): <Math tex="-2 - 1 = -3" />; <Math tex="-1" />. Day 3 (1,0,0): <Math tex="3 - 1 = 2" />; <Math tex="1" />.
      <Math tex="Z = [[-4, 0], [-3, -1], [2, 1]]" />.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="6. A model applies three weight matrices in a row: 784 inputs → 256 outputs, then 256 → 128, then 128 → 10, each with a bias. Give every weight shape and the total parameter count.">
    <p>
      Weights (256, 784), (128, 256), (10, 128). Parameters: <Math tex="784 \cdot 256 + 256 = 200{,}960" />;
      <Math tex="256 \cdot 128 + 128 = 32{,}896" />; <Math tex="128 \cdot 10 + 10 = 1{,}290" />. Total 235,146.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="7. Input (64, 784) through the three steps in problem 6. Write the shape after each step. Then do it for an input of shape (8, 20, 784).">
    <p>
      (64, 256) → (64, 128) → (64, 10). With the extra axis: (8, 20, 256) → (8, 20, 128) → (8, 20, 10). Only the last axis changes.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="8. A and B are both (128, 64). Give the shape of A Bᵀ, the number of entries, and the FLOPs to compute it. If 128 doubles to 256, by what factor does each grow?">
    <p>
      (128, 128); 16,384 entries; about <Math tex="2 \cdot 128 \cdot 64 \cdot 128 \approx 2.1" /> million FLOPs. Doubling
      to 256 quadruples the entries (65,536) and the FLOPs (about 8.4 million). Quadratic in <Math tex="n" />.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="9. A 1024 × 1024 matrix is written as B A with B of shape 1024 × 16 and A of shape 16 × 1024. How many numbers do B and A hold together, as a percentage of the full matrix? Compare the cost of (B A) x against B (A x) for one vector.">
    <p>
      <Math tex="2 \cdot 1024 \cdot 16 = 32{,}768" /> parameters against <Math tex="1024^2 = 1{,}048{,}576" />: about 3.1%.
      Forming <Math tex="BA" /> first costs <Math tex="1024 \cdot 16 \cdot 1024 \approx 16.8" /> million multiplications;
      <Math tex="B(A\mathbf{x})" /> costs <Math tex="16 \cdot 1024 + 1024 \cdot 16 = 32{,}768" />. About 500 times cheaper.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="10. Which of these broadcasts, and to what shape? (3, 4) + (4,); (3, 4) + (3,); (3, 4) + (3, 1); (3, 1) + (1, 4); (2, 3, 4) + (3, 4); (2, 3, 4) + (2, 4).">
    <p>
      (3, 4) yes; (3,) no (3 vs 4); (3, 1) yes → (3, 4); (3, 1)+(1, 4) yes → (3, 4), both stretch; (2, 3, 4)+(3, 4) yes → (2, 3, 4); (2, 4) no (4 vs 4 fine, then 2 vs 3 fails).
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="11. M = [[0, −1], [1, 0]]. Where do (1, 0) and (0, 1) go? Sketch the image of the unit square. What does this matrix do to the plane?">
    <p>
      Columns: (1, 0) → (0, 1) and (0, 1) → (−1, 0). The square turns a quarter turn anticlockwise. <Math tex="M" /> is a
      90° rotation. Apply it twice: <Math tex="M^2 = [[-1, 0], [0, -1]]" />, a half turn.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="12. GPT-2 small: d = 768, vocabulary 50,257, 12 layers. Estimate the parameters in the embedding matrix and in the two feed-forward matrices of one layer (768 → 3072 → 768). Then estimate forward-pass FLOPs per token for the feed-forward parts of all 12 layers.">
    <p>
      Embedding: <Math tex="50{,}257 \times 768 \approx 38.6" /> million. Feed-forward per layer:
      <Math tex="768 \cdot 3072 + 3072 + 3072 \cdot 768 + 768 \approx 4.72" /> million. All 12: about 56.7 million.
      FLOPs per token: 2 per parameter, about 113 million for the feed-forward blocks alone.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <h3>Code</h3>
  <QA q="13. Implement matmul with three nested loops. Check it against @ on random shapes, and make it raise a clear error when the inner dimensions differ.">
    <CodeBlock
      code={`import numpy as np

def matmul(A, B):
    m, n = A.shape
    n2, p = B.shape
    if n != n2:
        raise ValueError(f"inner dimensions differ: A is {m}x{n}, B is {n2}x{p}")
    C = np.zeros((m, p))
    for i in range(m):              # each row of A
        for j in range(p):          # each column of B
            for k in range(n):      # the dot product
                C[i, j] += A[i, k] * B[k, j]
    return C

rng = np.random.default_rng(0)
for m, n, p in [(2, 3, 2), (5, 1, 4), (3, 7, 3), (1, 6, 1)]:
    A, B = rng.normal(size=(m, n)), rng.normal(size=(n, p))
    assert np.allclose(matmul(A, B), A @ B)
try:
    matmul(np.zeros((2, 3)), np.zeros((2, 3)))
except ValueError as e:
    print(e)                        # inner dimensions differ: A is 2x3, B is 2x3
print("ok")`}
    />
  </QA>
