  <QA q="15. Write a two-layer network forward pass in NumPy for a batch, asserting the shape after every step. Then make it accept an input with an extra leading axis without changing any code.">
    <CodeBlock
      code={`import numpy as np

rng = np.random.default_rng(0)
W1, b1 = rng.normal(size=(4, 3)) * 0.5, np.zeros(4)      # (out, in)
W2, b2 = rng.normal(size=(2, 4)) * 0.5, np.zeros(2)

def forward(X):
    H = X @ W1.T + b1;      assert H.shape == X.shape[:-1] + (4,)
    H = np.maximum(H, 0);   assert H.shape == X.shape[:-1] + (4,)
    Y = H @ W2.T + b2;      assert Y.shape == X.shape[:-1] + (2,)
    return Y

print(forward(rng.normal(size=(32, 3))).shape)        # (32, 2)
print(forward(rng.normal(size=(8, 128, 3))).shape)    # (8, 128, 2)  same code, extra leading axis`}
    />
  </QA>
  <QA q="16. Compute all pairwise cosine similarities between 1,000 random 64-dimensional embeddings in one matrix expression. Verify one entry by hand with the dot product formula, and confirm the diagonal is all ones.">
    <CodeBlock
      code={`import numpy as np

rng = np.random.default_rng(0)
E = rng.normal(size=(1000, 64))
E_unit = E / np.linalg.norm(E, axis=1, keepdims=True)    # (1000, 64) / (1000, 1): broadcasting
S = E_unit @ E_unit.T                                    # (1000, 1000): every pair at once

i, j = 3, 17
by_hand = (E[i] @ E[j]) / (np.linalg.norm(E[i]) * np.linalg.norm(E[j]))
assert np.isclose(S[i, j], by_hand)
assert np.allclose(np.diag(S), 1.0)                      # every vector is perfectly similar to itself
assert np.allclose(S, S.T)                               # similarity is symmetric
print(S.shape, S[i, j])`}
    />
