  <h2 id="mistakes">Common mistakes</h2>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Using <code>*</code> for matrix multiplication.</strong> It is element-wise. For two square matrices it
      runs without error and returns the wrong thing. Matmul is <code>@</code>.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Reading shapes as (columns, rows).</strong> Shape is always (rows, columns), and a linear layer’s weight
      is (out, in). If a shape error names dimensions that “should” match, check which axis is which before anything
      else.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Mixing (n,), (n, 1) and (1, n).</strong> <code>x @ x</code> is a scalar for shape (n,), but a (n, 1) times
      (n, 1) is an error and a (1, n) times (n, 1) is a (1, 1) matrix. When something is “almost” a number but prints
      with brackets, this is why. Use <code>.reshape</code> or <code>[:, None]</code> deliberately.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Broadcasting a per-row vector without adding an axis.</strong> <code>Z - Z.mean(axis=1)</code> fails for
      a non-square <code>Z</code> and silently does the wrong thing for a square one. Use
      <code>keepdims=True</code>.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Assuming <Math tex="AB = BA" />.</strong> Order carries meaning: which side you multiply on decides
      whether you act on rows or columns. Keep formulas in their written order.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Building a product of thin matrices in full.</strong> Computing <Math tex="BA" /> as a full matrix when
      you only need <Math tex="BA\mathbf{x}" /> throws away the whole point of writing it as two thin pieces. Bracket as
      <Math tex="B(A\mathbf{x})" />.
    </p>
  </Callout>

